    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 71 tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe requirement was that all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) return an RFC 7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`, keeping the same HTTP status codes.\n\nWhat I found and changed:\n\n- **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** already emitted `ProblemDetail` bodies (Spring auto-sets the `application/problem+json` content type) for bean-validation 400s and other exceptions. But its catch-all `Exception` handler would have turned any controller-thrown rejection into a 500.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1`** was the only place producing 400/409/429, and it did so via bare `new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.\u2026)` returns \u2014 empty bodies, no problem+json.\n\nChanges:\n1. Added a `@ExceptionHandler(ResponseStatusException.class)` to `ExceptionControllerAdvice` that builds a `ProblemDetail` (`type` = request URL, `title` = exception name, `status`, `detail` = the reason) and returns it with the original status. This sits ahead of the 500 catch-all.\n2. Converted all nine bare rejection returns in `OwnerRestControllerV1` (telephone/email/address/postcode/future-date \u2192 400; identity-duplicate/city-capacity \u2192 409; daily-limit \u2192 429; plus the update telephone 400) to `throw new ResponseStatusException(status, \"detail\")` with descriptive messages.\n\n`NOT_FOUND` (404) returns were left untouched since they're outside the specified 400/409/429 set. The new `Cp59Tests` (missing-city \u2192 problem+json 400) and all pre-existing tests pass.",
